        "h": { "type": "heading", "settings": { "text": "How gear ratio works" } },
        "t1": { "type": "text", "settings": { "text": "<p>Diff ratio is the number of turns of the pinion for one turn of the wheels. A 3.73:1 ratio means the tail shaft turns 3.73 times for every turn of the wheel. A numerically higher ratio (say 4.11) gives more acceleration and higher cruising rpm; a lower ratio (say 3.08) gives a quieter, more economical highway car.</p>" } },
        "t2": { "type": "text", "settings": { "text": "<p>Fitting bigger tyres raises the effective ratio and dulls the car. Going from a 30 inch to a 35 inch tyre changes the final drive by around 17 percent, so a 3.07 would want roughly a 3.55 to bring the feel back. Bigger tyres also make the speedo read slow and the odometer under-read.</p>" } },
